From d905e14ecf5e08f34c25532418aa537c5d1db800 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alp Lab AB <contact@alplab.ai>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 17:41:10 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] rzv2n-dev, i2c: rzg2l_riic: enable P06/P07 pull-up, fix RIIC
 clock and restart races, and fix long-read tail-byte corruption

Bench root cause (E1M-V2M103, U-Boot bcf29d98, RIIC8/BRD_I2C, P06/P07):
"i2c md 0x1e 0x19 1" on RIIC8 failed with ICSR2=0x0a (STOP|AL,
arbitration lost) on the repeated START. Two independent, each
individually-sufficient fixes were bench-proven from the U-Boot prompt:

(a) SoC-internal pull-up bias on P06/P07. Linux already programs
    PUPD_H (port 0) = 0x03030000 (byte2=P06, byte3=P07, pull-up)
    while U-Boot left the register at its POR value of 0 -- no
    pull-up at all. Writing 0x03030000 from the U-Boot prompt made
    the read return reliably.
(b) A slower SCL. Setting ICMR1 CKS=5 (write 0x50) also made reads
    work.

Root cause of (b): riic_set_clock() (drivers/i2c/rzg2l_riic.c) is one
CKS/ICBRH/ICBRL table shared by every SoC this driver builds for,
tuned for a 50 MHz RIIC input clock. R9A09G056/057 (RZ/V2N, RZ/V2H)
run RIIC off a 100 MHz input instead (Linux uses ICBRL/ICBRH 0x0d/0x07
at CKS=3 for its own 400 kHz bus on the same clock), so U-Boot's
"100 kHz" config (CKS=3, unmodified) actually runs the bus at roughly
double rate -- bench-measured ~220 kHz.

- board/renesas/rzv2n-dev/rzv2n-dev.c: next to the existing P06/P07
  PFC_20/PMC_20 function-select write in s_init(), RMW PUPD_H (port 0,
  PFC_BASE+0x1D04) to set bytes 2/3 (P06/P07) to 0x03 (pull-up),
  preserving bytes 0/1 (P04/P05) as-is.

- drivers/i2c/rzg2l_riic.c riic_set_clock(): for R9A09G056/057 builds
  only, STANDARD rate uses CKS(5) and FAST rate uses CKS(3) (was
  CKS(3)/CKS(1) unmodified). fSCL is proportional to (input clock /
  2^CKS) for fixed ICBRH/ICBRL, so one CKS step (3->4, 1->2) cancels
  the doubled input clock and restores the same nominal rate this
  table already hits elsewhere in the family at 50 MHz -- a SECOND
  step (4->5, 2->3) is then taken as deliberate margin for the loaded
  BRD_I2C bus, per the bench matrix below (not a second clock-doubling
  correction). Per the driver's own bit-rate formula, fSCL = (P0phi /
  2^CKS) / (m + n + 2), m/n = ICBRH/ICBRL bits[4:0] (the 0xE0 upper
  bits are a fixed must-write-1 pattern, not part of m/n), ignoring
  tr/tf (bus-loading-dependent, not computable here): at 100 MHz input
  with m=n=23 (STANDARD, register write value 0xE0|23 = 0xF7 for both
  ICBRH/ICBRL), CKS(4) computes to (100e6/16)/48 ~= 130.2 kHz (the
  restored-nominal rate, still loses bit 7 on this bus -- see the
  matrix below) and CKS(5) computes to (100e6/32)/48 ~= 65.1 kHz (this
  patch's value, bench-clean). For FAST rate (m=20, n=19), CKS(2)
  computes to (100e6/4)/41 ~= 609.8 kHz (restored-nominal) and CKS(3)
  to (100e6/8)/41 ~= 304.9 kHz (this patch's value); FAST rate is not
  itself part of the bench matrix (only STANDARD/RIIC8 was exercised
  on the bench), carried by the same margin reasoning only. Other
  SoCs sharing this driver (R9A09G047, R9A08G045S) are untouched --
  their actual RIIC input clock is not established here.

Two further, smaller robustness fixes in the same file, generic to
every RIIC instance in the family (not gated to RIIC8), targeting the
exact AL failure signature above:

- riic_read_common(): after the offset-write TEND wait and before the
  repeated START, poll ICCR1 SDAI=1 (SDA released), bounded ~1 ms
  (raised from an initial ~100 us bound -- too tight to observe
  anything useful on the bench matrix runs below; a genuinely stuck
  slave still falls through to riic_send_start_cond()'s own
  RIIC_TIMEOUT rather than hanging here). Issuing the repeated START
  while a slave still holds SDA low from its offset-write ACK races
  the controller and can lose arbitration -- exactly the ICSR2=0x0a
  (STOP|AL) failure seen on the bench. Prints elapsed time + ICCR1 if
  the poll itself times out, so a bench run shows whether a failure
  coincided with SDA held low.

- riic_wait_for_icsr2(): its existing unconditional timeout printf
  (bit awaited, ICSR2, ICCR2) now also prints ICCR1, so a timeout here
  shows SDAI (bit 0) too -- the same signature the SDAI poll above
  guards against, now visible on every wait timeout, not only the
  repeated-START path.

- riic_check_busy(): ICSR2 AL/STOP/NACKF/START are sticky (cleared
  only by explicit software write, never by hardware on the next
  START), so a prior transaction's abort can leave one set and
  riic_wait_for_icsr2() on the NEXT transaction reads it as an
  immediate false event. Clear all four unconditionally before the
  busy-bus check. AL specifically also means the controller's internal
  state machine may itself be out of sync with the bus, so on AL run
  the same ICE=0+IICRST full reset/re-init already used for the
  BBSY-stuck recovery (0005) instead of leaving AL merely cleared.

Neither of (a)/(b) individually proven necessary AND sufficient beyond
the single bench pass each was validated on -- both are carried
together since each alone was independently sufficient there and they
are cheap and orthogonal. The SDAI-poll and stale-ICSR2-clear
additions are defensive hardening for the same failure class, not
separately bench-validated in isolation.

A third, separate defect in the same function group, found on a
follow-up bench pass reading more than a few bytes (E1M-V2M103
RIIC8/BRD_I2C, "i2c md 0x1e 0x00.1 0x10" on the DA9292 -- bytes at
offset 0x0D and 0x0E came back as 0x16/0x7F instead of the
independently-verified 0x96/0xFF, bit 7 cleared on both, reproducible):

- drivers/i2c/rzg2l_riic.c riic_i2c_raw_read(): the do-while loop read
  ICDRR for a byte FIRST and only set ICMR3.ACKBT (NACK) or ICCR2.SP
  (stop request) AFTER that read, in a separate post-loop code path
  for the final byte. With ICMR3.WAIT held set for the life of the
  RIIC instance (riic_init_setting() sets it once and this driver
  never clears it mid-transfer), the read of ICDRR is what releases
  the WAIT hold and commits whatever ACKBT/SP state is current at that
  instant onto the bus for the byte just consumed -- setting them
  AFTER that same read is one byte-time too late to affect this
  transfer. drivers/i2c/rz_riic.c riic_receive_data(), a separate,
  independently-correct RIIC driver already carried in-tree for other
  Renesas parts, does this the right way (set ACKBT/SP, then read
  ICDRR, on the final-byte iteration) -- this patch collapses the
  buggy split do-while/post-loop shape into one unified loop matching
  that same ordering, for every read length including the 1-byte case.

  This reorder is a REAL, independently-correct protocol fix, kept on
  its own merits and matching the manual's documented master-receive
  sequence -- but a follow-up bench matrix (E1M-V2M103, RIIC8/BRD_I2C,
  DA9292 0x1E) REFUTES it as the cause of the bit-7 loss above: a v4
  build without this reorder and a v5 build with it produced IDENTICAL
  results on the same burst read -- both still lose bit 7 on this bus.
  The actual mechanism is bus-speed margin (see the CKS fix above and
  the matrix immediately below), not protocol ordering.

Bench matrix (E1M-V2M103, RIIC8/BRD_I2C, DA9292 0x1E; BRD_I2C carries
~13 populated branches; external 2.2 kOhm pull-ups to the 1.8 V
rail are fitted, in addition to the SoC-internal pull-up this
patch also adds):

- CKS=4 (this patch's prior default through v5): single bursts longer
  than ~12 bytes lose bit 7 on bytes with bit7 set from about position
  13 onward -- position-dependent, not register-dependent (a 21-byte
  burst: offset 0x0d 0x96->0x16, 0x0e 0xff->0x7f, 0x12/0x13 0xff->0x7f,
  0x14 0xac->0x0c). 8-byte reads are clean at CKS=4.
- CKS=5 (this patch): a 16-byte AND a single 32-byte i2c-read burst are
  both completely clean.
- CKS=3: fails outright (STOP detected, -110) -- recovery kicks in,
  not a usable fallback.
- WAIT held off (ICMR3 0x30) and ICBRL forced to 0xff: still corrupt
  at the faster rates -- rules out WAIT/ICBRL as the mechanism.

Root mechanism of the bit-7 loss at CKS=4 is NOT confirmed -- Linux
drives the same physical bus at 400 kHz, IRQ-driven, with clean reads,
which does not obviously square with "just needs a slower clock". A
scope capture of the affected bytes is still pending; CKS(5) is
carried here on bench evidence (it is clean on every burst length
tried, CKS=4 is not), not on a proven timing model.

Signed-off-by: Alp Lab AB <contact@alplab.ai>
---
 board/renesas/rzv2n-dev/rzv2n-dev.c |  26 +++++
 drivers/i2c/rzg2l_riic.c            | 175 ++++++++++++++++++++++++----
 2 files changed, 180 insertions(+), 21 deletions(-)

diff --git a/board/renesas/rzv2n-dev/rzv2n-dev.c b/board/renesas/rzv2n-dev/rzv2n-dev.c
index 689c30624..dbd6d1d23 100644
--- a/board/renesas/rzv2n-dev/rzv2n-dev.c
+++ b/board/renesas/rzv2n-dev/rzv2n-dev.c
@@ -57,6 +57,24 @@ DECLARE_GLOBAL_DATA_PTR;
 #define	PFC_23				(PFC_BASE + 0x048C)
 #define PFC_PWPR                        (PFC_BASE + 0x3C04)
 
+/*
+ * ALP: P06/P07 (RIIC8 / BRD_I2C) internal pull-up bias. PUPD_H is a
+ * per-port, byte-per-pin register distinct from the PMC/PFC function-
+ * select block above -- pins 4-7 of a port live in its high word
+ * (PUPD_H), one byte each (0=off, 3=pull-up; matches the encoding Linux
+ * already uses on this same net). Bench-confirmed value for port 0
+ * (E1M-V2M103, U-Boot bcf29d98): Linux programs PUPD_H = 0x03030000
+ * (byte2=P06, byte3=P07, both pull-up; bytes 0/1 = P04/P05, left 0)
+ * while U-Boot leaves this register at its POR value of 0 -- i.e. NO
+ * pull-up on P06/P07 in U-Boot today. Without it, "i2c md 0x1e 0x19 1"
+ * on RIIC8 fails with ICSR2=0x0a (STOP|AL, arbitration lost) on the
+ * repeated START; writing this same 0x03030000 from the U-Boot prompt
+ * made the read return reliably. Only port 0 is needed here (RIIC8 is
+ * the only bus U-Boot masters before Linux takes over BRD_I2C), so this
+ * is a fixed address, not a per-port macro.
+ */
+#define	PFC_P0_PUPD_H			(PFC_BASE + 0x1D04)
+
 #define ICU_IPTSR_REG			0x10400060
 
 /* CPG */
@@ -117,6 +135,14 @@ void s_init(void)
 	*(volatile u32 *)PFC_20  = (*(volatile u32 *)PFC_20 & 0x00FFFFFF) | (0x01 << 28) | (0x01 << 24);
 	*(volatile u8 *)PMC_20   |= (0x03) << 6;	/* P07,P06 multiplexed function	*/
 
+	/* ALP: enable internal pull-up on P06/P07 (RIIC8/BRD_I2C), matching
+	 * what Linux already does on the same net -- see PFC_P0_PUPD_H
+	 * above for why U-Boot needs this too (bench: arbitration-lost
+	 * reads without it). RMW: only bytes 2/3 (P06/P07), bytes 0/1
+	 * (P04/P05) preserved as-is. */
+	*(volatile u32 *)PFC_P0_PUPD_H =
+		(*(volatile u32 *)PFC_P0_PUPD_H & 0x0000FFFFu) | (0x03u << 16) | (0x03u << 24);
+
 	/* I2C0 (RIIC0) on P30/P31 -- ALP on-module EEPROM bus (E1M_I2C0),
 	 * read in board_late_init() to identify the SoM. Func.1 = RIIC0. */
 	*(volatile u32 *)PFC_23  = (*(volatile u32 *)PFC_23 & ~0xFFu) | (0x01 << 4) | (0x01 << 0);
diff --git a/drivers/i2c/rzg2l_riic.c b/drivers/i2c/rzg2l_riic.c
index dc223fa09..f8712bf9f 100644
--- a/drivers/i2c/rzg2l_riic.c
+++ b/drivers/i2c/rzg2l_riic.c
@@ -20,6 +20,7 @@
 #include <dm/device_compat.h>
 #include <linux/bitops.h>
 #include <linux/delay.h>
+#include <time.h>
 
 #if defined(CONFIG_R9A09G057) || defined(CONFIG_R9A09G056) || defined(CONFIG_R9A09G047) || defined(CONFIG_R9A08G045S)
 #define RIIC_ICCR1	0x00
@@ -208,14 +209,77 @@ static int riic_set_clock(struct udevice *dev, int clock)
 	case 100000:
 		riic_clear_bit(priv, ICFER_FMPE, RIIC_ICFER);
 		riic_clear_bit(priv, ICMR1_CKS_MASK, RIIC_ICMR1);
+#if defined(CONFIG_R9A09G057) || defined(CONFIG_R9A09G056)
+		/*
+		 * ALP: this CKS/ICBRH/ICBRL table is shared by every SoC that
+		 * compiles this driver, tuned for a 50 MHz RIIC input clock.
+		 * R9A09G056/057 (RZ/V2N, RZ/V2H) run RIIC off a 100 MHz input
+		 * instead -- bench-confirmed on E1M-V2M103 by leaving CKS(3)
+		 * unmodified and measuring the "100 kHz" bus at ~220 kHz.
+		 * fSCL is proportional to (input clock / 2^CKS) for fixed
+		 * ICBRH/ICBRL, so incrementing CKS by one from the unmodified
+		 * table (3->4) exactly cancels a doubled input clock in that
+		 * dominant term, restoring the same nominal rate this table
+		 * already hits elsewhere in the family at 50 MHz.
+		 *
+		 * CKS(5) here -- one step slower than that restored-nominal
+		 * value -- is deliberate margin, not a second clock-doubling
+		 * correction: bench matrix on the loaded BRD_I2C (DA9292 at
+		 * 0x1E, ~13 populated branches, external 2.2 kOhm pull-ups
+		 * to the 1.8 V rail plus the SoC-internal pull-up) shows
+		 * CKS(4) loses bit 7 on bytes at/after roughly byte 13 of a
+		 * burst read (e.g. a 21-byte burst: offset 0x0d 0x96->0x16,
+		 * 0x0e 0xff->0x7f, 0x12/0x13 0xff->0x7f, 0x14 0xac->0x0c --
+		 * position-dependent, not register-dependent; short reads of
+		 * <=8 bytes are clean even at CKS(4)), while a 16-byte AND a
+		 * single 32-byte burst are both clean at CKS(5). CKS(3) fails
+		 * outright (STOP detected, -110, recovery kicks in) -- not a
+		 * usable fallback.
+		 *
+		 * Per the driver's own bit-rate formula, fSCL = (P0phi /
+		 * 2^CKS) / (m + n + 2) where m/n are ICBRH/ICBRL bits[4:0]
+		 * (the 0xE0 upper bits below are a fixed must-write-1 pattern,
+		 * not part of m/n) -- ignoring tr/tf, which are bus-loading-
+		 * dependent and not computable here. At 100 MHz input with
+		 * m = n = 23 (register write value ICBRH = ICBRL = 0xE0 | 23
+		 * = 0xF7): CKS(4) computes to (100e6/16)/48 ~= 130.2 kHz (the
+		 * restored-nominal value that still loses bit 7 on this bus);
+		 * CKS(5) computes to (100e6/32)/48 ~= 65.1 kHz, the bench-
+		 * clean value used below. Root mechanism of the bit-7 loss at
+		 * the faster rate is not confirmed (Linux drives the same bus
+		 * at 400 kHz IRQ-driven with clean reads) -- scope capture is
+		 * pending; CKS(5) is carried on bench evidence, not a proven
+		 * timing model.
+		 */
+		riic_set_bit(priv, ICMR1_CKS(5), RIIC_ICMR1);
+#else
 		riic_set_bit(priv, ICMR1_CKS(3), RIIC_ICMR1);
+#endif
 		riic_write(priv, ICBRH_RESERVED | 23, RIIC_ICBRH);
 		riic_write(priv, ICBRL_RESERVED | 23, RIIC_ICBRL);
 		break;
 	case 400000:
 		riic_clear_bit(priv, ICFER_FMPE, RIIC_ICFER);
 		riic_clear_bit(priv, ICMR1_CKS_MASK, RIIC_ICMR1);
+#if defined(CONFIG_R9A09G057) || defined(CONFIG_R9A09G056)
+		/*
+		 * ALP: same 100 MHz-input doubled-clock correction as the
+		 * 100 kHz case above (CKS 1->2 restores nominal), plus the
+		 * same one-step margin (2->3) for the loaded BRD_I2C bus --
+		 * see the 100 kHz case for the bench matrix and the fSCL
+		 * formula this is derived from. With m = 20, n = 19
+		 * (ICBRH = 0xE0 | 20 = 0xF4, ICBRL = 0xE0 | 19 = 0xF3):
+		 * CKS(2) computes to (100e6/4)/41 ~= 609.8 kHz (restored-
+		 * nominal); CKS(3) computes to (100e6/8)/41 ~= 304.9 kHz,
+		 * this patch's value. FAST rate is not part of the bench
+		 * matrix above (only STANDARD/100 kHz RIIC8 was exercised on
+		 * the bench) -- carried by the same margin reasoning, not
+		 * independently bench-validated.
+		 */
+		riic_set_bit(priv, ICMR1_CKS(3), RIIC_ICMR1);
+#else
 		riic_set_bit(priv, ICMR1_CKS(1), RIIC_ICMR1);
+#endif
 		riic_write(priv, ICBRH_RESERVED | 20, RIIC_ICBRH);
 		riic_write(priv, ICBRL_RESERVED | 19, RIIC_ICBRL);
 		break;
@@ -246,6 +310,25 @@ static int riic_check_busy(struct udevice *dev)
 		4.7 us(Sm) and 1.3 us(Fm). */
 
 	ulong start, timeout = (ulong)RIIC_TIMEOUT;
+
+	/*
+	 * ALP: ICSR2 AL/STOP/NACKF/START are sticky -- only cleared by an
+	 * explicit write, never by hardware on the next START -- so a prior
+	 * transaction's abort can leave one set here and riic_wait_for_icsr2()
+	 * on the transaction we are about to start reads it as an immediate
+	 * (false) event. AL is worse than stale: "arbitration lost" means the
+	 * controller's internal state machine may itself be out of sync with
+	 * the bus, so clearing the bit alone would leave that state behind --
+	 * run the same ICE=0+IICRST full reset/re-init used for the BBSY-stuck
+	 * recovery below instead of leaving AL sticky. STOP/NACKF/START get
+	 * the cheap clear unconditionally either way.
+	 */
+	if (riic_read(priv, RIIC_ICSR2) & ICSR2_AL) {
+		printf("ALP: riic stale AL (arbitration lost) from a prior transfer, resetting controller\n");
+		riic_init_setting(dev, I2C_SPEED_STANDARD_RATE);
+	}
+	riic_clear_bit(priv, ICSR2_AL | ICSR2_STOP | ICSR2_NACKF | ICSR2_START, RIIC_ICSR2);
+
 	start = get_timer(0);
 
 	do {
@@ -331,11 +414,15 @@ static int riic_wait_for_icsr2(struct udevice *dev, unsigned short bit)
 
 	/*
 	 * ALP: always visible on the bench, not gated behind a DEBUG build
-	 * -- shows which wait timed out. Failure path only, so the rate is
-	 * fine.
+	 * -- shows which wait timed out. ICCR1 added alongside ICSR2/ICCR2
+	 * so a timeout here also shows SDAI (bit 0) -- whether the failure
+	 * coincides with the slave still holding SDA low, the same signature
+	 * the SDAI poll below guards against on the repeated-START path.
+	 * Failure path only, so the rate is fine.
 	 */
-	printf("%s: timeout!(bit = %x icsr2 = %x, iccr2 = %x)\n", __func__,
-		bit, riic_read(priv, RIIC_ICSR2), riic_read(priv, RIIC_ICCR2));
+	printf("%s: timeout!(bit = %x icsr2 = %x, iccr2 = %x, iccr1 = %x)\n", __func__,
+		bit, riic_read(priv, RIIC_ICSR2), riic_read(priv, RIIC_ICCR2),
+		riic_read(priv, RIIC_ICCR1));
 
 	return -ETIMEDOUT;
 }
@@ -412,32 +499,54 @@ static int riic_i2c_raw_read(struct udevice *dev, struct i2c_msg *msg)
 {
 	struct riic_priv *priv = dev_get_priv(dev);
 
-	int dummy_read = 1;
 	int ret = 0;
 	int index = 0;
 
+	/*
+	 * ALP: dummy read -- the first RDRF after ADDR+R is ACKed reflects
+	 * the address phase, not a data byte (matches drivers/i2c/rz_riic.c
+	 * riic_receive_data() and Linux drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-riic.c
+	 * riic_rdrf_isr()'s RIIC_INIT_MSG dummy read). Reading ICDRR here
+	 * only arms the WAIT-held clock for the real first byte.
+	 */
+	ret = riic_wait_for_icsr2(dev, ICSR2_RDRF);
+	if (ret < 0)
+		return ret;
+	riic_read(priv, RIIC_ICDRR);
+
+	/*
+	 * ALP: root cause of the tail-byte corruption seen on long reads
+	 * (bench: E1M-V2M103 RIIC8/BRD_I2C, 16-byte DA9292 dump -- 2 of the
+	 * last 3 bytes came back with bit 7 stuck clear). The previous
+	 * split do-while/post-loop shape read ICDRR for the final byte
+	 * FIRST and only set ACKBT (NACK) and ICCR2.SP (stop request)
+	 * AFTER that read -- but WAIT holds SCL until the ICDRR read, and
+	 * that same read is what releases the hold and commits whatever
+	 * ACKBT/SP state is current at that instant onto the bus. Setting
+	 * them after the read is one byte-time too late for this transfer;
+	 * every multi-byte read was acking (not NACKing) its true last byte
+	 * and requesting STOP only after the fact, per the RIIC
+	 * master-receive flowchart and the proven-correct sibling driver
+	 * drivers/i2c/rz_riic.c riic_receive_data(), both of which set
+	 * ACKBT/SP BEFORE the ICDRR read that consumes the final byte. One
+	 * unified loop below does that: arm NACK+STOP request ahead of the
+	 * read on the last iteration, plain ACK (continue) otherwise.
+	 */
 	do {
 		ret = riic_wait_for_icsr2(dev, ICSR2_RDRF);
 		if (ret < 0)
 			return ret;
 
-		msg->buf[index] = riic_read(priv, RIIC_ICDRR);
-		if (dummy_read)
-			dummy_read = 0;
-		else
-			index++;
-		riic_set_receive_ack(priv, 1);
-	} while (index < (msg->len - 1));
+		if (index == msg->len - 1) {
+			riic_clear_bit(priv, ICSR2_STOP, RIIC_ICSR2);
+			riic_set_bit(priv, ICCR2_SP, RIIC_ICCR2);
+			riic_set_receive_ack(priv, 0);
+		} else {
+			riic_set_receive_ack(priv, 1);
+		}
 
-	ret = riic_wait_for_icsr2(dev, ICSR2_RDRF);
-	if (ret < 0)
-		return ret;
-
-	riic_clear_bit(priv, ICSR2_STOP, RIIC_ICSR2);
-	riic_set_bit(priv, ICCR2_SP, RIIC_ICCR2);
-
-	msg->buf[index++] = riic_read(priv, RIIC_ICDRR);
-	riic_set_receive_ack(priv, 0);
+		msg->buf[index++] = riic_read(priv, RIIC_ICDRR);
+	} while (index < msg->len);
 
 	return ret;
 }
@@ -566,6 +675,30 @@ static int riic_read_common(struct udevice *dev, struct i2c_msg *msg, u32 addr,
 		if (ret < 0)
 			goto force_exit;
 
+		/*
+		 * ALP: a repeated START issued while the slave still holds SDA
+		 * low from the offset-write ACK races the controller and can
+		 * lose arbitration (bench: E1M-V2M103 RIIC8/BRD_I2C, ICSR2 =
+		 * 0x0a = STOP|AL on this exact restart). Wait for SDA to be
+		 * released first; bounded so a genuinely stuck slave still
+		 * falls through to riic_send_start_cond()'s own RIIC_TIMEOUT
+		 * rather than hanging here.
+		 */
+		{
+			ulong t0 = timer_get_us();
+			ulong elapsed;
+
+			while (!(riic_read(priv, RIIC_ICCR1) & ICCR1_SDAI) &&
+			       (ulong)(timer_get_us() - t0) < 1000) {
+				/* busy-poll -- 1 ms bound, no sleep granularity to spare */
